The smash product of two neighbourhood systems is the neighbourhood system on the disjoint sum whose neighbourhoods are the product of the two masters together with the proper product neighbourhoods. The master is that product of masters; it is nonempty because the first master is nonempty, and it is a neighbourhood by the master clause. If either of two smash neighbourhoods is the master, their intersection is the other, since each factor lies inside its master. If both are proper, a consistency witness equal to the master would force a factor to be a master, so the witness is a proper product whose factors lie in the two intersections, and those intersections remain proper. Every smash neighbourhood is contained in the product of the masters.
